(()=>{
const modules=[
{title:'1. The Instructor as a Professional',topic:'Professional role',summary:'Know the limits, responsibilities, ethics, safety obligations, and professional behaviors of an Instructor I.',points:[
'Instructor I primarily delivers instruction from prepared lesson plans and course materials. The key word is prepared.',
'Adaptation means making the lesson fit the audience, environment, equipment, or local conditions while preserving the required objective. Fundamental redesign or rewriting objectives belongs at a higher instructional level.',
'Prepare before class: know the lesson, verify resources, inspect the training area, understand safety requirements, and be able to perform any skill you will demonstrate.',
'Model the behavior you expect: punctuality, correct PPE, respectful communication, accurate information, and consistent application of rules.',
'Stay within your competence. If you do not know an answer, say so, identify a reliable source, and follow up rather than guessing.',
'Training creates legal and ethical duties. Follow current standards, AHJ policy, lesson-plan safety requirements, and manufacturer instructions; document the training accurately.',
'Protect learner privacy and test information. Attendance, scores, remediation, medical/accommodation information, and personnel issues should only be shared as authorized.',
'An instructor is not simply a subject-matter expert. Knowing the skill and being able to create learning are separate competencies.'
],trap:'Test trap: when two answers look correct, prefer the option that preserves the prepared objective, follows AHJ policy, protects safety, and treats students consistently.'},
{title:'2. Principles of Learning',topic:'Learning',summary:'Understand how adults learn, the three learning domains, motivation, practice, feedback, and barriers to learning.',points:[
'Cognitive domain = knowledge and thinking. Psychomotor domain = physical skill performance. Affective domain = attitudes, values, and beliefs.',
'Adult learners engage better when the content is relevant to their job, when prior experience is respected, and when they understand why the lesson matters.',
'Learning objectives tell the student what successful performance looks like. Instruction and evaluation should both align with the objective.',
'Practice must be purposeful. Repeating an incorrect skill only reinforces the error; instructors should detect errors early and provide corrective feedback.',
'Feedback is most useful when it is timely, specific, behavior-focused, and gives the learner a next action.',
'Motivation may be intrinsic (personal mastery, pride, competence) or extrinsic (promotion, certification, recognition). The instructor can support both without humiliating or threatening learners.',
'Learners arrive with different experience, ability, language, confidence, and physical capability. Adjust delivery and support while maintaining the required performance standard.',
'Retention improves when learners retrieve information repeatedly over time and apply it in realistic contexts rather than only rereading notes.'
],trap:'Test trap: “more lecture” is rarely the best fix for a psychomotor performance problem. Demonstration, coached practice, feedback, and another attempt usually fit the objective better.'},
{title:'3. Instructional Planning',topic:'Planning',summary:'Read and use prepared lesson plans, identify objectives and prerequisites, sequence content, and make allowed adaptations.',points:[
'A lesson plan provides continuity and consistency between instructors and deliveries. It is the roadmap to the stated learning outcome.',
'A well-written performance objective identifies who performs, the observable behavior, the conditions, and the required degree/standard. A common memory aid is ABCD: Audience, Behavior, Condition, Degree.',
'Terminal objectives describe the final expected outcome. Enabling objectives are the smaller knowledge or skill steps that support the final outcome.',
'Before class, identify prerequisites. A student who lacks a prerequisite may require remediation before the planned lesson can be successful or safe.',
'Sequence instruction logically: known to unknown, simple to complex, and prerequisite skills before integrated evolutions.',
'Estimate time for introduction, presentation/demonstration, application/practice, evaluation, breaks, setup, and cleanup.',
'Instructor I may adapt the method, examples, group size, room arrangement, or equipment to fit local needs when the objective remains intact and the course/AHJ permits it.',
'Have contingencies for missing equipment, audiovisual failure, weather, or changes in class size. A backup plan protects continuity.'
],trap:'Test trap: changing a learning objective because it is inconvenient is not an Instructor I adaptation.'},
{title:'4. Instructional Materials & Equipment',topic:'Materials',summary:'Select, inspect, operate, and use instructional aids so they support—not distract from—the objective.',points:[
'Choose media because it improves learning, not because it is impressive. The objective determines the aid.',
'Inspect props, tools, simulators, PPE, projectors, displays, speakers, and demonstration devices before students arrive.',
'Use equipment that matches the lesson and the equipment learners will actually use when possible.',
'Visuals should be readable, uncluttered, accurate, and visible from the learner area. Do not overload slides with text.',
'Introduce an aid at the logical point in the lesson, explain what students should notice, then transition back smoothly.',
'Handouts can support note-taking or job performance, but distributing them at the wrong time can pull attention away from the instructor.',
'Have a low-tech backup for critical content when practical. A failed projector should not end the class.',
'Use copyrighted or licensed material only as permitted by the owner, publisher, AHJ, or applicable license.'
],trap:'Test trap: the best instructional aid is the one that directly supports the objective and can be used safely and reliably in that environment.'},
{title:'5. The Learning Environment',topic:'Environment',summary:'Organize classrooms, labs, apparatus floors, and outdoor training areas for safety, access, attention, and learning.',points:[
'Before class, evaluate lighting, noise, temperature/weather, seating, sight lines, ventilation, distractions, power needs, exits, and accessibility.',
'Seating arrangement should fit the method: rows may support lecture, a U-shape may support discussion, and open work areas may be needed for skills.',
'Inspect the training area for physical hazards and control them before instruction begins. Safety issues take priority over schedule.',
'Outdoor evolutions require weather monitoring, hydration/rest planning as appropriate, communications, boundaries, and a plan for changing conditions.',
'Learners should know emergency procedures and any training-ground safety rules before hazardous or complex activity begins.',
'Reasonable accommodations should improve access to instruction or testing without changing an essential performance standard unless the AHJ authorizes otherwise.',
'Control avoidable distractions. Position learners so they can see demonstrations and hear commands.',
'Maintain a respectful environment in which questions are safe to ask and correction is focused on performance, not personal embarrassment.'
],trap:'Test trap: if the environment is unsafe, the correct answer is usually to correct the hazard, relocate, delay, or stop—not simply warn students and continue.'},
{title:'6. Instructional Delivery & Communication',topic:'Delivery',summary:'Present prepared lessons clearly, choose appropriate methods, question learners, and maintain continuity.',points:[
'A common four-step teaching sequence is Preparation, Presentation, Application, Evaluation.',
'Preparation gains attention, establishes relevance, activates prior knowledge, states objectives, and sets expectations.',
'Lecture efficiently delivers information; discussion uses learner experience and reasoning; demonstration shows how a skill is performed; guided practice lets students perform with coaching.',
'Ask questions throughout the lesson to check understanding. Open questions promote explanation; closed questions can quickly verify a specific fact.',
'After asking a question, allow enough wait time for students to think. Do not answer your own question immediately.',
'Communication includes words, tone, pace, volume, gestures, eye contact, positioning, and listening.',
'Correct disruptive behavior early and professionally. Use the least disruptive effective intervention and follow AHJ policy.',
'If conditions change, adjust pace, examples, media, grouping, or method while maintaining class continuity and the objective.'
],trap:'Test trap: talking more is not always teaching better. The best method is the one aligned to what the student must know or do.'},
{title:'7. Student Interaction, Coaching & Remediation',topic:'Coaching',summary:'Guide learners, correct errors, handle questions and behavior, and support struggling students without lowering standards.',points:[
'Establish rapport without sacrificing standards. Respect, consistency, competence, and clear expectations build credibility.',
'Correct the behavior or performance, not the person. Specific feedback is more useful than labels such as “bad job.”',
'Remediation should target the identified deficiency. Break a complex skill into components, re-demonstrate as needed, coach practice, then re-evaluate according to policy.',
'A learner who asks a question may reveal a gap shared by the entire class. Clarify the objective before deciding whether to answer immediately or defer an off-topic issue.',
'Do not let one experienced student dominate discussion or intimidate less-experienced learners.',
'Use examples that connect to the learners’ assignments and experience, but do not replace approved content with war stories.',
'When performance standards apply, encouragement does not mean changing the standard. Support the learner in reaching it.',
'Document formal remediation or failed evaluations when required by the AHJ.'
],trap:'Test trap: choose individualized corrective instruction before punishment when the problem is a skill or knowledge deficiency rather than misconduct.'},
{title:'8. Skills-Based Training Beyond the Classroom',topic:'Skills training',summary:'Teach physical skills and evolutions safely through demonstration, practice, supervision, and feedback.',points:[
'Before a skill evolution, confirm objectives, prerequisites, PPE, equipment, staffing, communications, hazards, safety controls, and evaluation criteria.',
'Demonstrate the skill correctly. Learners should first see the desired whole performance, then the instructor can break it into teachable steps.',
'Explain critical safety steps before practice and reinforce them during the evolution.',
'Place instructors where they can observe critical actions and intervene before a learner creates an unacceptable hazard.',
'Stop unsafe activity immediately. Corrective instruction can occur after the hazard is controlled.',
'Give students enough supervised repetitions to build correct performance. Quality practice matters more than simply completing a number of repetitions.',
'Increase complexity only after foundational skills are reliable. Integrated evolutions depend on earlier skills.',
'Finish with a debrief: what happened, why it happened, how performance relates to the objective, and what should improve next time.'
],trap:'Test trap: safety is an active instructor responsibility during training; it cannot be delegated away by telling students to “be careful.”'},
{title:'9. Testing & Evaluation',topic:'Testing',summary:'Administer fair written, oral, and performance evaluations, understand basic test quality, protect security, and give feedback.',points:[
'Validity asks whether an evaluation measures the intended knowledge or skill. Reliability asks whether it produces consistent results under consistent conditions.',
'Objectivity reduces the influence of evaluator opinion. Clear rubrics, answer keys, and performance checklists improve objectivity.',
'Formative assessment occurs during learning and guides improvement. Summative evaluation judges achievement at the end of a unit or course.',
'Criterion-referenced evaluation compares performance with a defined standard, not with how other students performed.',
'Administer tests consistently: same instructions, time limits, authorized materials, environment, and scoring process unless an approved accommodation applies.',
'During a certification evaluation, the evaluator observes and scores; coaching the candidate changes the validity and fairness of the evaluation.',
'Protect test items, answer keys, candidate work, and results according to policy.',
'After evaluation, provide feedback that is permitted by the testing authority and direct remediation to the objective or task that was not achieved.'
],trap:'Test trap: if a test question appears ambiguous, follow the formal challenge process; the proctor should not reinterpret or coach the candidate toward an answer.'},
{title:'10. Records, Reports & Scheduling',topic:'Records',summary:'Create complete, accurate, secure training records and manage the routine logistics of instructional delivery.',points:[
'Training records commonly document who attended, what was taught, when and where it occurred, who instructed, objectives or course identification, and completion/evaluation results as required by the AHJ.',
'Record facts accurately and promptly. Do not alter a score or attendance record to help a learner or department look better.',
'Follow AHJ retention and records-management requirements. Training records may later support certification, qualification, investigation, or litigation.',
'Protect personally identifiable and confidential information in both paper and electronic systems.',
'When correcting a record, use the approved correction process so the record remains auditable.',
'Scheduling includes coordinating instructors, students, facilities, equipment, and required time while avoiding resource conflicts.',
'Communicate cancellations or schedule changes quickly through the approved channel.',
'After class, return equipment/materials, report damaged items or incidents, submit required results, and capture useful instructor notes for the next delivery.'
],trap:'Test trap: if it was not documented according to the AHJ process, you may not be able to prove it occurred.'}
];

const flashcards=[
['Professional role','What is the central job of an Instructor I?','Deliver and manage instruction using prepared lesson plans and course materials while maintaining safety and the stated objectives.'],
['Professional role','Adapt vs. modify: what is the key distinction?','Adaptation makes a prepared lesson fit local conditions or learners without fundamentally changing the objective; modification makes fundamental changes to the plan/objectives.'],
['Professional role','If you do not know the answer to a student question, what should you do?','Acknowledge it, use or identify a reliable source, and follow up rather than guessing.'],
['Professional role','What should an instructor model during training?','Professional conduct, correct PPE and procedures, respect, punctuality, preparation, safety, and accurate communication.'],
['Professional role','What is a subject-matter expert (SME)?','A person with deep expertise in a subject or task; being an SME does not automatically make someone an effective instructor.'],
['Professional role','What is the safest response when course content conflicts with current AHJ policy?','Stop and resolve the conflict through the proper authority before teaching or practicing the conflicting procedure.'],
['Professional role','Why is confidentiality important to an instructor?','Student scores, remediation, personnel information, and protected records should only be shared as authorized.'],
['Professional role','What is the best protection against “teaching from memory” errors?','Review and follow the current prepared lesson plan and approved references before class.'],
['Learning','Name the three learning domains.','Cognitive, psychomotor, and affective.'],
['Learning','Cognitive domain means…','Knowledge, comprehension, reasoning, and other mental processes.'],
['Learning','Psychomotor domain means…','Physical or manipulative skill performance.'],
['Learning','Affective domain means…','Attitudes, values, beliefs, and willingness to act.'],
['Learning','Why should an instructor explain relevance early?','Adults learn more effectively when they understand why the material matters to their job or goals.'],
['Learning','What makes feedback effective?','It is timely, specific, focused on observable performance, and provides a clear next action.'],
['Learning','Intrinsic motivation comes from…','Internal reasons such as mastery, pride, curiosity, or professional competence.'],
['Learning','Extrinsic motivation comes from…','External rewards or consequences such as certification, promotion, recognition, or requirements.'],
['Learning','What is transfer of learning?','Using knowledge or skills learned in training in a different or real-world situation.'],
['Learning','What is remediation?','Targeted additional instruction and practice addressing a learner’s specific deficiency.'],
['Planning','What is the main purpose of a lesson plan?','To guide consistent instruction toward stated learning objectives.'],
['Planning','ABCD objective: A stands for…','Audience.'],
['Planning','ABCD objective: B stands for…','Behavior—the observable action or performance.'],
['Planning','ABCD objective: C stands for…','Condition—the situation, equipment, or information provided.'],
['Planning','ABCD objective: D stands for…','Degree—the required level or standard of performance.'],
['Planning','What is a terminal objective?','The final expected learning or performance outcome.'],
['Planning','What is an enabling objective?','A smaller prerequisite objective that helps the learner reach the terminal objective.'],
['Planning','A strong instructional sequence usually moves from…','Known to unknown and simple to complex, with prerequisites before integrated skills.'],
['Planning','Can Instructor I rewrite an objective because the class is short on time?','No. Preserve the required objective; adjust within allowed limits or resolve the scheduling problem through the proper authority.'],
['Planning','Why create a contingency plan?','To maintain safe instruction and continuity if equipment, weather, facilities, or class conditions change.'],
['Materials','What should determine selection of an instructional aid?','The learning objective and the needs of the lesson—not novelty.'],
['Materials','When should audiovisual equipment be tested?','Before students arrive or before it is needed in the lesson.'],
['Materials','What is the best response to a projector failure?','Use a prepared backup method that still supports the objective.'],
['Materials','Why should an instructor inspect demonstration props?','To confirm they are safe, functional, complete, and appropriate for the lesson.'],
['Materials','What makes a visual aid effective?','It is accurate, readable, uncluttered, visible, and directly supports the point being taught.'],
['Materials','When should a teaching aid be introduced?','At the logical point where it supports the content or demonstration.'],
['Materials','What is a common problem with distributing handouts too early?','Students may read ahead and stop attending to the presentation.'],
['Materials','How should copyrighted materials be used?','Only as permitted by the owner, license, publisher, AHJ, or applicable law/policy.'],
['Environment','What environmental factors should be checked before class?','Lighting, noise, temperature/weather, seating, sight lines, ventilation, distractions, exits, access, and safety hazards.'],
['Environment','What should determine seating arrangement?','The instructional method and learner activity.'],
['Environment','What takes priority if a training area is unsafe?','Correct, control, relocate, delay, or stop the activity before continuing.'],
['Environment','What is an accommodation intended to do?','Improve access to learning or evaluation without improperly changing essential performance requirements.'],
['Environment','Why identify exits and emergency procedures?','So students and instructors can respond appropriately if an actual emergency occurs during training.'],
['Environment','What is a good approach to avoidable distractions?','Remove, reduce, or control them so attention stays on the learning task.'],
['Environment','How should weather affect outdoor training?','Monitor conditions and adapt, relocate, postpone, or stop as required for safety and objectives.'],
['Environment','What kind of environment encourages questions?','A respectful, psychologically safe environment where correction focuses on performance rather than humiliation.'],
['Delivery','What are the four common steps of instructional delivery?','Preparation, Presentation, Application, Evaluation.'],
['Delivery','What happens during Preparation?','Gain attention, explain relevance, connect prior knowledge, state objectives, and set expectations.'],
['Delivery','What method is especially appropriate for teaching a physical skill?','Demonstration followed by supervised learner practice.'],
['Delivery','What method makes strong use of learner experience?','Guided discussion.'],
['Delivery','What is an open question?','A question that asks the learner to explain, analyze, compare, or describe rather than give only a short fixed answer.'],
['Delivery','Why use wait time after asking a question?','It gives learners time to process and formulate an answer.'],
['Delivery','What is class continuity?','A logical, uninterrupted flow of instruction toward the objective even when adjustments are needed.'],
['Delivery','How should disruptive behavior be handled?','Early, professionally, with the least disruptive effective intervention and according to policy.'],
['Coaching','What is the best focus when correcting a learner?','The observable behavior or performance—not the person’s character.'],
['Coaching','What should remediation target?','The specific knowledge or skill deficiency identified by assessment or observation.'],
['Coaching','Does helping a learner mean lowering the performance standard?','No. Adapt support and instruction while maintaining required outcomes.'],
['Coaching','How should an off-topic question be handled?','Answer briefly if useful and time allows, or park it for later so the lesson objective and continuity are preserved.'],
['Coaching','What is a good use of learner experience?','Connect approved content to realistic examples while preventing personal stories from replacing the lesson.'],
['Coaching','When may remediation need documentation?','Whenever required by the AHJ, course, certification process, or evaluation policy.'],
['Skills training','What should happen before a hands-on evolution starts?','Confirm objectives, prerequisites, PPE, equipment, staffing, hazards, safety controls, communications, and evaluation criteria.'],
['Skills training','How should a new psychomotor skill commonly be demonstrated?','Show the correct whole performance, then break it into teachable steps and supervise practice.'],
['Skills training','What should happen when a student creates an unsafe condition?','Stop the activity and control the hazard immediately, then provide corrective instruction.'],
['Skills training','Why supervise repetitions?','To prevent practice from reinforcing incorrect or unsafe technique.'],
['Skills training','When should complexity increase?','After the foundational skill is performed reliably enough to support the next level.'],
['Skills training','What is the purpose of a post-evolution debrief?','Connect observed performance to objectives, reinforce lessons, and identify improvements.'],
['Skills training','Where should instructors position during a skill evolution?','Where they can observe critical actions and intervene before unacceptable hazards develop.'],
['Skills training','What is more important than a fixed number of repetitions?','Correct, purposeful practice with feedback.'],
['Testing','What is validity?','How well a test or evaluation measures what it is intended to measure.'],
['Testing','What is reliability?','The consistency of evaluation results under consistent conditions.'],
['Testing','What is objectivity?','Scoring that minimizes personal judgment or bias, usually through clear criteria, keys, or checklists.'],
['Testing','What is formative assessment?','Assessment during learning used to guide improvement.'],
['Testing','What is summative evaluation?','Evaluation at the end of a unit/course used to judge achievement.'],
['Testing','What is criterion-referenced evaluation?','Comparing learner performance to a defined standard or criterion.'],
['Testing','What should a certification evaluator avoid?','Coaching or providing hints during the evaluation.'],
['Testing','How should a suspected ambiguous test item be handled?','Use the authorized challenge or appeal process; do not reinterpret the test for the candidate.'],
['Testing','Why is test security important?','It protects fairness, validity, certification integrity, and confidential exam content.'],
['Testing','What should feedback after an evaluation address?','Performance compared with the objective or standard and the next remediation step, within testing-policy limits.'],
['Records','What should a training record establish?','Who trained, what was taught, when/where it occurred, who instructed, and completion/evaluation details required by the AHJ.'],
['Records','Why should records be completed promptly?','Accuracy declines with delay, and the record may be needed to prove qualification or completion.'],
['Records','How should an incorrect record be fixed?','Use the AHJ-approved correction process so the change remains traceable.'],
['Records','Who decides how long records are kept?','Applicable law, AHJ policy, certification requirements, and records-management procedures.'],
['Records','How should electronic training records be protected?','With authorized access, appropriate security, backups, and compliance with AHJ policy.'],
['Records','What should happen after a training injury or equipment damage?','Provide needed care/control, then complete required incident, injury, equipment, and training documentation.'],
['Records','What resources must scheduling coordinate?','Students, instructors, facility, time, equipment, and any required support resources.'],
['Records','Why are instructor notes after class useful?','They capture logistical issues and allowed adaptations that can improve the next delivery without changing required objectives.']
];

const Q=(topic,q,choices,a,why)=>({topic,q,choices,a,why});
const questions=[
Q('Professional role','An Instructor I is assigned a lesson plan that has already been approved. Which action is most appropriate?',['Rewrite the objectives to match the instructor’s preferred emphasis','Teach from memory and use the plan only if questions arise','Review the plan, identify needed local adaptations, and preserve the stated objectives','Replace the evaluation with an easier one'],2,'Instructor I works from prepared materials and may adapt delivery to local conditions without fundamentally changing the approved learning outcomes.'),
Q('Professional role','A student asks a technical question and the instructor is unsure of the answer. What is the BEST response?',['Give the most likely answer so the class keeps moving','Tell the student the question is outside the course and ignore it','Acknowledge the limit, identify a reliable source, and follow up','Ask another student to guess'],2,'Professional credibility is protected by accurate information. It is better to verify than to guess.'),
Q('Professional role','Which behavior BEST demonstrates instructor professionalism?',['Using personal war stories instead of the lesson plan','Applying safety and performance expectations consistently to all learners','Allowing senior firefighters to skip required practice','Changing scores to reward effort'],1,'Professional instructors are prepared, consistent, fair, respectful, and safety-focused.'),
Q('Professional role','A course requires a tool the instructor has never used. What should the instructor do BEFORE teaching the skill?',['Teach it from the manufacturer photo','Practice privately until comfortable, regardless of qualification','Obtain competent instruction/qualification and be able to perform the skill correctly','Ask the first student to demonstrate it'],2,'An instructor should be competent in the skill or use a qualified SME/instructor rather than improvise beyond competence.'),
Q('Professional role','Which action most clearly creates an ethical problem?',['Providing the same test instructions to each candidate','Documenting a failed performance according to policy','Giving a friend hints during a certification evaluation','Referring a difficult question to an SME'],2,'Coaching one candidate undermines fairness, validity, and certification integrity.'),
Q('Professional role','A learner’s remediation record should be shared with:',['Anyone in the department who asks','Only persons authorized by policy or legitimate need','The entire class to prevent the same error','Outside instructors for comparison'],1,'Training records can include sensitive personnel information and should be protected.'),
Q('Professional role','What is the MOST important reason an instructor should use current approved course materials?',['They reduce setup time','They help maintain accuracy, consistency, and alignment with adopted requirements','They eliminate the need for instructor preparation','They guarantee all students will pass'],1,'Current approved materials support consistency and accurate coverage of required outcomes.'),
Q('Professional role','A veteran firefighter says a required safety step is unnecessary because “we never do that here.” The instructor should:',['Delete the step to keep credibility with the class','Teach the approved requirement and resolve any policy conflict through the proper authority','Let each student choose which method to use','End the entire course'],1,'An Instructor I should not casually remove required safety or performance content.'),
Q('Professional role','Which statement BEST describes the relationship between subject expertise and instructional ability?',['A subject expert is automatically an effective instructor','Instructional ability matters only for classroom topics','Subject expertise and the ability to create learning are related but distinct competencies','Instructor skill is unnecessary when the lesson plan is detailed'],2,'Knowing a subject and teaching it effectively are different competencies.'),
Q('Professional role','During a practical evolution, an unsafe condition develops. The instructor’s FIRST priority is to:',['Complete the objective before the scheduled break','Document the issue before intervening','Control or stop the unsafe condition','Ask the class to vote on whether to continue'],2,'Safety takes priority over schedule or continuity.'),

Q('Learning','A firefighter correctly explains the parts of a ladder but cannot raise it. Which learning domain needs the MOST work?',['Cognitive','Psychomotor','Affective','Administrative'],1,'The deficiency is physical performance, so the psychomotor domain is primary.'),
Q('Learning','A learner can perform a skill but repeatedly chooses not to follow a safety practice because they do not value it. Which domain is MOST involved?',['Cognitive','Psychomotor','Affective','Mechanical'],2,'Values, attitudes, and willingness are affective-domain issues.'),
Q('Learning','Which opening is MOST likely to increase adult learner engagement?',['“This is on the test, so pay attention.”','“By the end of this drill you will be able to make a faster, safer decision you use on working fires.”','“I have taught this for 20 years.”','“The manual says we have to cover this.”'],1,'Relevance and clear job value support adult motivation.'),
Q('Learning','A student repeatedly practices an incorrect knot without feedback. The biggest instructional concern is that:',['The learner will become bored','Incorrect practice can reinforce the error','The learner may become too confident in the instructor','The class will finish early'],1,'Practice strengthens what is practiced, correct or incorrect.'),
Q('Learning','Which feedback is MOST effective?',['“That was terrible.”','“Good job.”','“Your hand position allowed the tool to slip; reset your grip here and try the step again.”','“You should already know this.”'],2,'Effective feedback is specific, observable, and actionable.'),
Q('Learning','A firefighter studies because they want to master the position even though no promotion is available. This is primarily:',['Extrinsic motivation','Intrinsic motivation','Negative transfer','Summative evaluation'],1,'Mastery and personal competence are intrinsic motivators.'),
Q('Learning','Which approach BEST supports retention for a certification test?',['Reread the same chapter continuously in one sitting','Use spaced review and repeated retrieval over several days','Avoid practice questions until the night before','Study only the easiest topics first'],1,'Spaced practice and retrieval strengthen recall better than massed rereading.'),
Q('Learning','A learner understands a hose-loading procedure in class and later performs it correctly on the apparatus floor. This is an example of:',['Transfer of learning','Norm-referenced grading','Affective resistance','Test bias'],0,'Transfer is application of learning in another context.'),
Q('Learning','A student fails one component of a skill. The BEST remediation plan is to:',['Repeat the entire course regardless of cause','Identify the deficient component, reteach/practice it, and re-evaluate according to policy','Lower the passing standard','Remove the student from the class immediately'],1,'Remediation should target the identified deficiency while preserving required standards.'),
Q('Learning','Which statement about learner differences is BEST?',['All adults learn best with the same method','Different experience and ability may require adjustments in instruction while outcomes remain consistent','Experienced firefighters do not need objectives','Accommodations always change the performance standard'],1,'Instruction can be adapted to learners while required outcomes remain in place.'),

Q('Planning','The main purpose of a prepared lesson plan is to:',['Allow the instructor to avoid studying','Provide a consistent roadmap toward learning objectives','Replace all instructor judgment','Serve only as an attendance record'],1,'Lesson plans organize content, methods, resources, and evaluation around objectives.'),
Q('Planning','In the ABCD objective format, the “B” identifies the:',['Background','Behavior','Benchmark source','Break period'],1,'B = observable behavior.'),
Q('Planning','“Given a charged 1¾-inch line, the firefighter will advance the line 50 feet without losing control.” The phrase “Given a charged 1¾-inch line” describes the:',['Audience','Behavior','Condition','Degree'],2,'The condition describes what is provided or the circumstances of performance.'),
Q('Planning','A final objective stating what a learner must accomplish at the end of the lesson is a:',['Terminal objective','Enabling objective','Distractor','Formative note'],0,'Terminal objectives describe the final expected outcome.'),
Q('Planning','Before teaching an advanced search evolution, the instructor discovers several learners cannot properly don SCBA. The BEST action is to:',['Continue because SCBA is not today’s objective','Address the prerequisite deficiency before the advanced evolution','Rewrite the advanced objective','Allow partners to don the SCBA for them'],1,'Prerequisites must be present for safe and successful advanced learning.'),
Q('Planning','Which sequence is generally BEST for introducing a complex skill?',['Complex to simple','Unknown to known','Simple components before integrated performance','Evaluation before instruction'],2,'Instruction commonly progresses from simple to complex and prerequisites to integration.'),
Q('Planning','A training room is smaller than the layout shown in the lesson plan. The objective and content remain unchanged. The Instructor I may generally:',['Cancel every time','Adapt the seating arrangement to fit the facility safely','Delete the skill objective','Create a new certification standard'],1,'Room arrangement is a typical adaptation when it does not change required outcomes.'),
Q('Planning','A lesson objective requires a 24-foot extension ladder, but only a 14-foot roof ladder is available. The BEST action is to:',['Change the objective to the roof ladder','Skip the objective but mark it complete','Obtain the required resource or resolve the issue before the lesson/evaluation','Tell learners to imagine the extension section'],2,'The instructor should not falsely claim an objective was met or fundamentally change it without authority.'),
Q('Planning','Which item belongs in a strong pre-class contingency plan?',['A way to continue if a critical projector fails','A list of students who are expected to fail','An alternate passing score','Permission to ignore weather'],0,'Contingency planning protects continuity when equipment or conditions change.'),
Q('Planning','When estimating course time, the instructor should include:',['Presentation only','Presentation and testing only','Setup, introduction, instruction, practice, evaluation, breaks, and cleanup as applicable','Only the time printed on slides'],2,'Complete scheduling includes the full instructional process and logistics.'),

Q('Materials','An instructor selects a thermal imager video because it directly illustrates the lesson objective. This is an example of selecting media based on:',['Novelty','Learning need','Cost only','Instructor preference only'],1,'Instructional aids should be chosen because they support the objective.'),
Q('Materials','When is the BEST time to discover that a demonstration prop is damaged?',['During the student performance','After the class','During pre-class inspection','During final evaluation'],2,'Pre-class inspection allows hazards and failures to be corrected before learners use the equipment.'),
Q('Materials','A slide contains eight dense paragraphs in small font. The primary problem is:',['It is too colorful','It is unlikely to be readable and may compete with the instructor','It contains too few words','Slides should never contain text'],1,'Visual aids should be readable and support rather than overwhelm the presentation.'),
Q('Materials','The projector fails halfway through a critical lesson. The BEST response is to:',['End the class immediately','Use a prepared backup method that still meets the objective','Skip the objective','Spend the rest of the period repairing the projector'],1,'A backup method preserves continuity and objectives.'),
Q('Materials','When using a training video, the instructor should FIRST consider whether it:',['Is longer than the lecture','Directly supports the objective and is accurate/current enough for the intended use','Has dramatic music','Was used by another department'],1,'Media must support learning and be accurate.'),
Q('Materials','Why should an instructor rehearse with unfamiliar audiovisual equipment?',['To impress students','To reduce technical interruptions and confirm the aid works as intended','To eliminate the need for a lesson plan','To make the class longer'],1,'Familiarity improves reliability and continuity.'),
Q('Materials','A handout contains the answers to questions the instructor plans to ask during the introduction. The BEST distribution time may be:',['Before class so students can read the answers','After the introductory questioning, if that timing supports the plan','Never','Only after the course ends'],1,'Timing materials prevents them from undermining the learning activity.'),
Q('Materials','A demonstration device is safe but positioned where half the class cannot see it. The BEST correction is to:',['Continue and tell students to imagine it','Reposition learners/device or repeat the demonstration so all can observe critical steps','Reduce the objective','Only teach the front row'],1,'All learners need appropriate access to the demonstration.'),
Q('Materials','Which is the BEST reason to use the same model of equipment learners use on duty?',['It is always cheaper','It improves realism and transfer when it meets the lesson objective and is available','It prevents all mistakes','It replaces evaluation'],1,'Job-relevant equipment supports transfer of learning.'),
Q('Materials','An instructor wants to copy an entire commercial instructor manual and post it publicly. The BEST action is to:',['Do it because fire training is educational','Check copyright/license permissions and use only what is authorized','Remove the publisher name and post it','Ask a student to upload it instead'],1,'Educational use does not automatically permit wholesale public copying of copyrighted material.'),

Q('Environment','Before an indoor class, the instructor notices loud apparatus maintenance directly outside the room. What is the BEST response?',['Speak louder for the entire class','Reduce the distraction by relocating, rescheduling, closing barriers, or coordinating the work','Ignore it because firefighters work around noise','Remove the evaluation'],1,'Control avoidable environmental barriers to learning.'),
Q('Environment','Which seating arrangement is MOST likely to support an interactive group discussion?',['A U-shape or arrangement where participants can see one another','Students facing a blank wall','Learners dispersed into separate rooms','No seating regardless of class length'],0,'Discussion benefits from visual access and interaction.'),
Q('Environment','A severe thunderstorm approaches an outdoor ladder evolution. The BEST action is to:',['Continue until lightning is visible directly overhead','Follow the safety/weather plan and relocate, delay, or stop as required','Speed up the evolution','Let individual students decide whether to continue'],1,'Changing weather can create unacceptable hazards and should trigger the safety plan.'),
Q('Environment','Which issue should be corrected BEFORE a practical skill session?',['A minor spelling error on an optional handout','An uncontrolled trip hazard in the student work area','An instructor’s preferred chair is unavailable','The class asks too many questions'],1,'Physical hazards should be controlled before activity begins.'),
Q('Environment','The primary reason to identify emergency exits before training is to:',['Meet a slide-count requirement','Support safe evacuation if an actual emergency occurs','Increase test difficulty','Prevent students from leaving during breaks'],1,'Emergency procedures are part of a safe learning environment.'),
Q('Environment','A student requires an authorized accommodation for a written test. The instructor should:',['Deny it because all students must be identical','Provide the approved accommodation while preserving the essential standard and test security','Lower the passing score automatically','Give the student the answer key'],1,'Fair access and consistent essential standards can coexist.'),
Q('Environment','During a demonstration, students in the back cannot hear. The BEST first adjustment is to:',['Continue because the lesson plan says lecture','Change position, volume, grouping, or sound support so they can receive the instruction','Remove the back-row students','Skip the demonstration'],1,'The environment should be adapted so learners can access instruction.'),
Q('Environment','What is the BEST way to handle avoidable phone interruptions during a class?',['Publicly confiscate every phone','Set clear expectations and address disruptions professionally according to policy','Ignore any interruption','End class after the first ring'],1,'Clear expectations and professional behavior management support learning.'),
Q('Environment','Which statement BEST describes a safe learning environment?',['One with no risk at all','One in which hazards are identified and controlled appropriately for the training activity','One where students sign a waiver','One where only experienced firefighters participate'],1,'Fire training can involve managed risk, but hazards must be appropriately controlled.'),
Q('Environment','A room is too hot and students are visibly losing concentration. The BEST action is to:',['Continue unchanged because comfort is irrelevant','Adjust temperature/ventilation, take an appropriate break, or relocate while preserving the lesson','Fail inattentive students','Increase slide speed'],1,'Physical conditions affect learning and may need adaptation.'),

Q('Delivery','Which sequence correctly lists the common four-step instructional method?',['Presentation, Application, Preparation, Evaluation','Preparation, Presentation, Application, Evaluation','Evaluation, Preparation, Application, Presentation','Application, Preparation, Evaluation, Presentation'],1,'The common sequence is Preparation → Presentation → Application → Evaluation.'),
Q('Delivery','Which activity belongs primarily in the Preparation step?',['Scoring the final practical','Explaining why the topic matters and stating objectives','Collecting equipment after class','Issuing certificates'],1,'Preparation establishes relevance, readiness, and expectations.'),
Q('Delivery','Which method is BEST suited to teaching how to force a door with a Halligan?',['Lecture only','Demonstration followed by supervised practice','Silent reading only','Unstructured debate'],1,'Psychomotor skills require learners to see and then perform the skill.'),
Q('Delivery','Which question is MOST open-ended?',['“Is the PASS device activated?”','“What factors would make you change your search pattern in this room?”','“Is two-in/two-out a requirement?”','“Did you check the door?”'],1,'Open questions encourage explanation and reasoning.'),
Q('Delivery','After asking a difficult question, the instructor should:',['Immediately answer it to prevent silence','Allow appropriate wait time for learners to think','Select the same student every time','Tell students the answer is obvious'],1,'Wait time improves the chance of thoughtful learner responses.'),
Q('Delivery','The instructor’s voice is clear, but they stand behind a pillar while demonstrating a knot. Which communication issue is MOST important?',['Vocabulary','Learner sight line/nonverbal positioning','Exam reliability','Record retention'],1,'Physical positioning is part of instructional communication.'),
Q('Delivery','A student begins a side conversation that is distracting others. The BEST response is usually to:',['Ignore it regardless of impact','Use a professional, minimally disruptive correction and escalate according to policy if needed','Humiliate the student in front of the class','Cancel the course'],1,'Behavior should be addressed early and professionally.'),
Q('Delivery','Which method BEST uses the experience of a group of veteran firefighters to analyze a near miss?',['Guided discussion','Silent memorization','Timed spelling test','Lecture with no questions'],0,'Discussion can leverage learner experience and reasoning.'),
Q('Delivery','The class finishes a topic, but questions reveal widespread misunderstanding. The instructor should:',['Move on because the schedule matters most','Clarify/reteach using another appropriate method before relying on the misunderstood concept','Mark everyone absent','Lower the final test score requirement'],1,'Instruction should respond to evidence that objectives are not being reached.'),
Q('Delivery','Class continuity means the instructor should:',['Never change anything once class begins','Maintain a logical flow toward objectives while making necessary adjustments','Avoid all learner questions','Finish exactly on the planned minute even if unsafe'],1,'Continuity is preserved through deliberate adjustment, not rigid refusal to adapt.'),

Q('Coaching','A learner repeatedly misses one step of a hose deployment. What is the BEST first coaching action?',['Label the student as careless','Identify the specific error, explain/demonstrate the correction, and have the learner practice it','Lower the standard','Have another student complete the skill for them'],1,'Corrective coaching should focus on the specific performance deficiency.'),
Q('Coaching','Which statement is the BEST example of performance-focused feedback?',['“You are not very good at ladders.”','“Your halyard is outside the beams; reset it before the raise.”','“You always mess this up.”','“Try harder.”'],1,'Specific observable feedback is actionable and avoids personal labels.'),
Q('Coaching','An experienced learner dominates every discussion. The instructor should:',['Let the learner continue because experience equals authority','Use facilitation techniques that invite other learners and set limits professionally','Remove all discussion from the course','Ask the learner to teach the rest of class without preparation'],1,'The instructor manages participation so all learners can engage.'),
Q('Coaching','A learner asks a detailed question unrelated to the current objective. The BEST response is to:',['Allow it to consume the remaining class time','Briefly acknowledge it and defer it to a suitable time/resource so continuity is protected','Tell the learner never to ask questions','Change the course objective immediately'],1,'Off-topic issues can be parked without dismissing the learner.'),
Q('Coaching','Which action BEST supports a struggling learner while preserving standards?',['Provide additional guided practice and feedback','Automatically give a passing score for effort','Remove critical steps from the skill','Allow unsafe shortcuts'],0,'Support can be individualized without lowering required outcomes.'),
Q('Coaching','A learner’s poor performance appears to result from unclear instructions given by the instructor. The instructor should FIRST:',['Blame the learner','Clarify the instructions and reassess understanding/performance fairly','Document misconduct','Increase the passing standard'],1,'Instructional problems should be corrected before attributing failure to the learner.'),
Q('Coaching','A firefighter makes an error during formative practice. The instructor should generally:',['Use the error as an opportunity for timely correction and another attempt','Record an automatic certification failure','Ignore it because only the final test matters','End practice to avoid embarrassment'],0,'Formative practice is specifically the time to identify and correct errors.'),
Q('Coaching','What is the BEST use of an instructor “war story”?',['Replace approved content entirely','Briefly illustrate an approved learning point when accurate and relevant','Show that the instructor has more experience than the class','Avoid teaching objectives'],1,'Experience can enrich instruction but should support, not displace, approved content.'),
Q('Coaching','A learner becomes frustrated during remediation. The instructor should:',['Use sarcasm to motivate them','Maintain respect, break the task down, reinforce progress, and continue within policy','Lower the standard','Publicly compare them with the best student'],1,'Professional coaching supports improvement without humiliation.'),
Q('Coaching','When is formal documentation of remediation MOST appropriate?',['Only when the instructor remembers','When required by AHJ/course/certification policy or when the performance outcome requires a record','Never, because it may embarrass the student','Only for perfect performance'],1,'Required remediation and evaluation records should be documented accurately.'),

Q('Skills training','Before learners begin a forcible-entry evolution, the instructor should FIRST ensure:',['Only that the prop is available','Objectives, PPE, equipment, safety controls, instructor coverage, and prerequisites are in place','The fastest student goes first','The evaluation is hidden'],1,'Hands-on evolutions require deliberate pre-use safety and instructional preparation.'),
Q('Skills training','A useful sequence for demonstrating a new physical skill is to:',['Show only the individual steps and never the whole skill','Show the correct complete performance, then break it into teachable steps before student practice','Let students discover the method without guidance','Evaluate before demonstrating'],1,'Seeing the whole performance creates a mental model; stepwise instruction then supports skill acquisition.'),
Q('Skills training','During practice, a learner begins an action that could injure another student. The instructor should:',['Wait to see if the learner self-corrects','Stop the unsafe action immediately','Allow the evolution to finish for realism','Only write a note for the debrief'],1,'Immediate hazard control takes precedence.'),
Q('Skills training','Why should instructors watch repeated practice closely?',['To make learners nervous','To ensure repetitions reinforce correct and safe performance','To shorten the class','To eliminate debriefing'],1,'Unsupervised incorrect repetition can build bad habits.'),
Q('Skills training','A learner cannot perform a complex evolution because a basic component skill is weak. The BEST action is to:',['Increase complexity','Return to the component skill, remediate it, then rebuild the evolution','Lower the objective','Ask another learner to cover the weak step'],1,'Complex performance depends on reliable component skills.'),
Q('Skills training','What should a post-evolution debrief focus on?',['Personal criticism','Performance compared with objectives, decisions, safety, and specific improvement points','Only the fastest completion time','Unrelated department gossip'],1,'Debriefing consolidates learning by linking observed performance to objectives.'),
Q('Skills training','Instructor positioning during a skill evolution should primarily allow the instructor to:',['Stay comfortable','Observe critical actions and intervene for safety','Avoid learner questions','Be out of sight'],1,'Position supports observation, coaching, and hazard intervention.'),
Q('Skills training','Which practice strategy is BEST?',['Many rapid repetitions regardless of technique','Correct repetitions with feedback and enough recovery to maintain quality','One attempt only','Practice without an objective'],1,'Quality, feedback-rich practice is more valuable than unchecked repetition.'),
Q('Skills training','A skill evolution becomes unsafe because wind changes conditions. The Instructor I should:',['Continue because the lesson plan is already approved','Adapt, relocate, delay, or stop according to the safety plan while preserving the objective when possible','Delete safety steps','Allow each team to make its own rule'],1,'Changing conditions require active risk management.'),
Q('Skills training','A learner completes the skill correctly but cannot explain a critical safety reason required by the objective. The instructor should:',['Pass the learner because the physical skill looked good','Evaluate against the full objective, including the required knowledge component','Remove the knowledge requirement','Ask another learner to answer for them'],1,'Evaluation must match the whole objective, not just one domain.'),

Q('Testing','A test has high validity when it:',['Produces the same score every time regardless of learning','Measures the knowledge or skill it is intended to measure','Contains many difficult questions','Uses only true/false items'],1,'Validity is alignment between the assessment and intended construct/objective.'),
Q('Testing','Reliability refers to:',['Whether the test is popular','Consistency of results under consistent conditions','Whether the test is difficult','The cost of the test'],1,'Reliability is consistency.'),
Q('Testing','Which practice MOST improves objectivity in a performance evaluation?',['Scoring from memory after all students finish','Using a clear standardized checklist/rubric','Allowing each evaluator to use personal standards','Changing criteria for experienced students'],1,'Clear standardized criteria reduce evaluator bias.'),
Q('Testing','A short quiz during a lesson used to decide whether reteaching is needed is primarily:',['Summative','Formative','Norm-referenced','Administrative'],1,'Formative assessment guides ongoing learning.'),
Q('Testing','A final certification practical is primarily:',['Formative practice','Summative evaluation','Intrinsic motivation','A lesson-plan adaptation'],1,'A final practical judges whether the required outcome has been achieved.'),
Q('Testing','Criterion-referenced scoring compares the candidate with:',['Other candidates','A defined performance standard','The instructor’s favorite student','Last year’s class average'],1,'Criterion-referenced evaluation uses a fixed standard.'),
Q('Testing','During a certification performance test, a candidate pauses and looks at the evaluator for help. The evaluator should:',['Demonstrate the next step','Provide only assistance specifically authorized by the testing procedure','Give a hint so the candidate can continue','Ask another candidate to help'],1,'Evaluation must be administered consistently and without unauthorized coaching.'),
Q('Testing','A candidate believes a written test item is ambiguous. The BEST response is to:',['Argue with the proctor until an answer is given','Follow the authorized examination challenge/appeal procedure','Use a textbook during the exam without permission','Leave the item blank and tell no one'],1,'Formal challenge procedures protect exam integrity and fairness.'),
Q('Testing','Why should answer keys and secure test items be controlled?',['To make instruction more mysterious','To protect fairness, validity, and certification integrity','To prevent instructors from studying','To increase test length'],1,'Test security prevents compromised assessments.'),
Q('Testing','After a student fails an objective, the BEST feedback identifies:',['The student’s personality problem','The specific performance gap and the authorized next step for remediation/retest','How other students scored','A lower passing score'],1,'Feedback should connect performance to criteria and next actions.'),
Q('Testing','An authorized accommodation provides extra test time. The instructor should:',['Announce the reason to the class','Provide the accommodation confidentially and follow testing policy','Give easier questions','Remove the passing score'],1,'Accommodations should be administered confidentially and as approved.'),
Q('Testing','A practical test requires five critical steps. The evaluator likes the candidate and overlooks one missed critical step. This primarily damages:',['Objectivity and fairness','Classroom lighting','Intrinsic motivation','Lesson sequencing'],0,'Personal favoritism undermines objective application of criteria.'),
Q('Testing','Which statement BEST distinguishes assessment from evaluation?',['Assessment can be used throughout learning; evaluation often judges achievement against an outcome','They are always identical words','Evaluation happens only before instruction','Assessment cannot involve skills'],0,'Formative assessments guide learning while evaluations commonly determine achievement.'),
Q('Testing','A performance checklist includes vague terms such as “does a good job.” The main weakness is:',['Too much reliability','Poorly defined scoring criteria','Too many conditions','Excessive validity'],1,'Vague criteria increase evaluator judgment and reduce objectivity/reliability.'),
Q('Testing','A written exam mostly asks trivia unrelated to the course objectives. Its major problem is:',['Reliability','Validity','Security','Scheduling'],1,'If the exam does not measure intended objectives, validity is weak.'),

Q('Records','Which set of information is MOST appropriate for a training record?',['Only the instructor’s name','Participants, course/topic, date/location, instructor, and required completion/evaluation information','Only the best score','Personal opinions about each student'],1,'Training records should establish what occurred and who completed it according to AHJ requirements.'),
Q('Records','Why should attendance be recorded accurately?',['It helps instructors remember names only','It may establish participation, qualification, certification, or compliance','It is optional when the class is small','It can be reconstructed years later from memory'],1,'Attendance can have operational, certification, and legal significance.'),
Q('Records','An instructor enters the wrong score in the training system. The BEST action is to:',['Delete the entire class','Use the AHJ-approved correction process so the record remains accurate and auditable','Ignore it if the student passed anyway','Create a second secret record'],1,'Records should be accurate and corrected through an authorized process.'),
Q('Records','Who determines how long training records must be retained?',['The individual student only','Applicable law, AHJ policy, certification and records-management requirements','The newest instructor','Whoever has storage space'],1,'Retention is governed by organizational and legal requirements.'),
Q('Records','Electronic student records should be:',['Open to anyone with the web link','Protected with authorized access and appropriate security','Stored only on a personal phone','Posted publicly for transparency'],1,'Training records may contain protected or sensitive information.'),
Q('Records','An injury occurs during training. After immediate care and hazard control, the instructor should:',['Avoid documenting it to reduce liability','Complete required injury/incident/training documentation and notifications','Change the attendance roster','Delete the lesson plan'],1,'Accurate incident documentation supports safety, investigation, and organizational requirements.'),
Q('Records','The primary goal of instructional scheduling is to:',['Fit as many topics as possible into one day','Coordinate learners, instructors, facilities, equipment, and time so instruction can occur safely and effectively','Avoid breaks','Keep the instructor busy'],1,'Scheduling coordinates resources and prevents conflicts.'),
Q('Records','A class is canceled because the training facility becomes unavailable. The instructor should:',['Say nothing until students arrive','Communicate the change promptly through the approved channel and update records/schedule as required','Mark everyone absent','Teach in an unsafe replacement area'],1,'Timely communication prevents confusion and unsafe improvised substitutions.'),
Q('Records','Why are accurate instructor notes after a course useful?',['They can replace formal records','They can identify logistics and allowed adaptations to improve future delivery','They allow objectives to be changed without approval','They eliminate the need for feedback'],1,'Post-course notes can improve future delivery while formal records remain separate.'),
Q('Records','A student completed only half of a required course but asks the instructor to mark full completion. The instructor should:',['Do it if the student is experienced','Record only what actually occurred and follow policy for make-up/completion','Ask the student to promise to finish later','Delete the course roster'],1,'Training documentation must be truthful and defensible.'),
Q('Records','Which record is MOST likely to support future proof that a firefighter met a qualification requirement?',['An unsigned social media post','An official training record completed according to AHJ policy','The instructor’s memory','A verbal statement from a classmate'],1,'Formal records provide reliable evidence of training and completion.'),
Q('Records','If a course uses controlled test materials, records should:',['Include photographs of all secure test items','Document results as required without unnecessarily exposing secure test content','Be posted publicly','Be destroyed immediately regardless of policy'],1,'Results and training documentation can be retained without compromising secure exam content.'),
Q('Records','What is the BEST reason to submit training reports promptly?',['To make the class look faster','To preserve accuracy and make qualification/status information current','To reduce the passing standard','To avoid student feedback'],1,'Prompt reporting supports accurate current records.'),
Q('Records','A department requires instructor signature and course number on completion records. The Instructor I should:',['Omit them if everyone knows who taught','Complete the required fields accurately','Invent a course number','Use a student signature instead'],1,'Required fields are part of the record’s integrity.'),
Q('Records','Which statement BEST reflects the legal significance of training records?',['They can never be requested outside the training division','They may later be reviewed during certification, investigation, administrative action, or litigation','They are only personal notes','They are unimportant once a class is over'],1,'Training records may become important evidence of what was taught and who was qualified.'),
];

const readiness=[
'I can explain the difference between adapting and fundamentally modifying a prepared lesson.',
'I can identify cognitive, psychomotor, and affective learning outcomes.',
'I can explain ABCD learning objectives and distinguish terminal from enabling objectives.',
'I can select an instructional method that matches what the learner must know or do.',
'I can set up a classroom or skill area and identify environmental/safety problems before instruction.',
'I can deliver a short prepared lesson without reading slides or losing the objective.',
'I can demonstrate a physical skill, supervise practice, correct errors, and stop unsafe actions.',
'I can explain formative vs. summative assessment, validity, reliability, objectivity, and criterion-referenced evaluation.',
'I can administer an evaluation consistently without coaching a candidate.',
'I can explain what belongs in a training record and how to protect confidential information.',
'I have rehearsed a 10–15 minute practical lesson out loud with another person.',
'I have scored at least 90% on a full 100-question timed practice exam.',
'I have reviewed every missed practice-test question by topic instead of only memorizing its answer.',
'I know which NFPA/DFPC edition and current testing process applies to my actual certification test.'
];

const storageKey='fireops-instructor1-v1';
let state={modules:{},known:{},weak:{},readiness:{},bestExam:0};
try{state={...state,...JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(storageKey)||'{}')}}catch(e){}
const save=()=>localStorage.setItem(storageKey,JSON.stringify(state));

// module rendering
const moduleHost=document.getElementById('instructorModules');
modules.forEach((m,i)=>{const done=!!state.modules[i]; const a=document.createElement('article');a.className='instructor-module'+(done?' complete':'');a.innerHTML=`<button class="module-head" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><span>${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><div><p>${m.topic}</p><h3>${m.title}</h3><small>${m.summary}</small></div><b>+</b></button><div class="module-body" hidden><ul>${m.points.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join('')}</ul><p class="module-trap"><strong>Exam focus:</strong> ${m.trap}</p><label class="module-complete"><input type="checkbox" ${done?'checked':''}> I can explain this module without notes.</label></div>`;
 const head=a.querySelector('.module-head'),body=a.querySelector('.module-body'); head.addEventListener('click',()=>{const open=head.getAttribute('aria-expanded')==='true';head.setAttribute('aria-expanded',String(!open));body.hidden=open;head.querySelector('b').textContent=open?'+':'–';});
 a.querySelector('input').addEventListener('change',e=>{state.modules[i]=e.target.checked;a.classList.toggle('complete',e.target.checked);save();updateReadiness();});moduleHost.appendChild(a);
});

document.querySelectorAll('[data-scroll]').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>document.querySelector(b.dataset.scroll)?.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})));

// flashcards
const topics=[...new Set(flashcards.map(x=>x[0]))]; const topicSel=document.getElementById('flashcardTopic'); topics.forEach(t=>{const o=document.createElement('option');o.value=t;o.textContent=t;topicSel.appendChild(o)});
let deck=[...Array(flashcards.length).keys()],pos=0,flipped=false,weakOnly=false;
const card=document.getElementById('flashcard'),front=document.getElementById('flashcardFront'),counter=document.getElementById('cardCounter'),stats=document.getElementById('flashcardStats');
function buildDeck(){const t=topicSel.value;deck=flashcards.map((_,i)=>i).filter(i=>(t==='all'||flashcards[i][0]===t)&&(!weakOnly||state.weak[i]));if(!deck.length&&weakOnly){weakOnly=false;deck=flashcards.map((_,i)=>i).filter(i=>t==='all'||flashcards[i][0]===t)}pos=0;flipped=false;renderCard()}
function renderCard(){if(!deck.length)return; const i=deck[pos],d=flashcards[i];card.classList.toggle('is-flipped',flipped);card.querySelector('.flashcard-side').textContent=flipped?'Answer':d[0];front.textContent=flipped?d[2]:d[1];card.querySelector('.flashcard-hint').textContent=flipped?'Click to show question':'Click to flip';counter.textContent=`${pos+1} / ${deck.length}`;const known=Object.keys(state.known).filter(k=>state.known[k]).length, weak=Object.keys(state.weak).filter(k=>state.weak[k]).length;stats.textContent=`${known} cards marked known • ${weak} cards in weak deck`}
card.addEventListener('click',()=>{flipped=!flipped;renderCard()});
document.getElementById('cardKnow').addEventListener('click',()=>{const i=deck[pos];state.known[i]=true;delete state.weak[i];save();nextCard()});
document.getElementById('cardAgain').addEventListener('click',()=>{const i=deck[pos];state.weak[i]=true;delete state.known[i];save();nextCard()});
function nextCard(){flipped=false;pos=(pos+1)%deck.length;renderCard()}
document.getElementById('shuffleCards').addEventListener('click',()=>{for(let i=deck.length-1;i>0;i--){const j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));[deck[i],deck[j]]=[deck[j],deck[i]]}pos=0;flipped=false;renderCard()});
document.getElementById('weakCards').addEventListener('click',()=>{weakOnly=!weakOnly;document.getElementById('weakCards').classList.toggle('active',weakOnly);buildDeck()});
document.getElementById('resetCards').addEventListener('click',()=>{state.known={};state.weak={};save();weakOnly=false;buildDeck()});topicSel.addEventListener('change',buildDeck);buildDeck();

// exam
let examOrder=[],answers=[],flags=[],examPos=0,seconds=5400,timerId=null;
const launch=document.getElementById('examLaunch'),workspace=document.getElementById('examWorkspace'),results=document.getElementById('examResults');
function shuffle(a){for(let i=a.length-1;i>0;i--){const j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));[a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]}return a}
function startExam(){examOrder=shuffle([...Array(questions.length).keys()]).slice(0,100);answers=Array(100).fill(null);flags=Array(100).fill(false);examPos=0;seconds=5400;launch.hidden=true;results.hidden=true;workspace.hidden=false;clearInterval(timerId);timerId=setInterval(()=>{seconds--;renderTimer();if(seconds<=0){clearInterval(timerId);gradeExam()}},1000);renderExam();renderTimer();workspace.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})}
function renderTimer(){const h=Math.floor(seconds/3600),m=Math.floor(seconds%3600/60),s=seconds%60;document.getElementById('examTimer').textContent=[h,m,s].map(x=>String(x).padStart(2,'0')).join(':')}
function renderExam(){const q=questions[examOrder[examPos]];document.getElementById('examProgress').textContent=`Question ${examPos+1} of 100`;document.getElementById('examAnswered').textContent=`${answers.filter(x=>x!==null).length} answered`;document.getElementById('examTopic').textContent=q.topic;document.getElementById('examQuestion').textContent=q.q;const host=document.getElementById('examChoices');host.innerHTML='';q.choices.forEach((c,i)=>{const b=document.createElement('button');b.type='button';b.className=answers[examPos]===i?'selected':'';b.innerHTML=`<span>${String.fromCharCode(65+i)}</span>${c}`;b.addEventListener('click',()=>{answers[examPos]=i;renderExam()});host.appendChild(b)});document.getElementById('examPrev').disabled=examPos===0;document.getElementById('examNext').disabled=examPos===99;document.getElementById('examFlag').classList.toggle('flagged',flags[examPos]);}
document.getElementById('startExam').addEventListener('click',startExam);document.getElementById('examPrev').addEventListener('click',()=>{if(examPos>0){examPos--;renderExam()}});document.getElementById('examNext').addEventListener('click',()=>{if(examPos<99){examPos++;renderExam()}});document.getElementById('examFlag').addEventListener('click',()=>{flags[examPos]=!flags[examPos];renderExam()});document.getElementById('submitExam').addEventListener('click',()=>{if(confirm(`Grade now? You have answered ${answers.filter(x=>x!==null).length} of 100 questions.`))gradeExam()});
function gradeExam(){clearInterval(timerId);let correct=0;const topicStats={};const misses=[];examOrder.forEach((qi,i)=>{const q=questions[qi];topicStats[q.topic]??={right:0,total:0};topicStats[q.topic].total++;if(answers[i]===q.a){correct++;topicStats[q.topic].right++}else misses.push({num:i+1,q,selected:answers[i]})});state.bestExam=Math.max(state.bestExam||0,correct);save();workspace.hidden=true;results.hidden=false;const passed=correct>=80;const weak=Object.entries(topicStats).sort((a,b)=>(a[1].right/a[1].total)-(b[1].right/b[1].total));results.innerHTML=`<div class="exam-score ${passed?'pass':'fail'}"><span>${passed?'PASSING PRACTICE SCORE':'BELOW TARGET'}</span><strong>${correct}%</strong><p>${passed?'You cleared the 80% Colorado-style target. Aim for 90%+ twice before the real test.':'Use the topic breakdown below before retaking a full exam.'}</p><button id="retakeExam" class="button primary" type="button">Take another randomized exam</button></div><div class="exam-topic-results"><h3>Topic breakdown</h3>${weak.map(([t,s])=>`<div><span>${t}</span><b>${s.right}/${s.total} (${Math.round(s.right/s.total*100)}%)</b></div>`).join('')}</div><div class="exam-review"><h3>Review missed questions (${misses.length})</h3>${misses.map(x=>`<details><summary>${x.num}. ${x.q.q}</summary><p><strong>Your answer:</strong> ${x.selected===null?'No answer':x.q.choices[x.selected]}</p><p><strong>Best answer:</strong> ${x.q.choices[x.q.a]}</p><p>${x.q.why}</p><span>${x.q.topic}</span></details>`).join('')}</div>`;document.getElementById('retakeExam').addEventListener('click',startExam);results.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});updateReadiness()}

// readiness
const rHost=document.getElementById('readinessChecklist');readiness.forEach((r,i)=>{const l=document.createElement('label');l.innerHTML=`<input type="checkbox" ${state.readiness[i]?'checked':''}><span>${r}</span>`;l.querySelector('input').addEventListener('change',e=>{state.readiness[i]=e.target.checked;save();updateReadiness()});rHost.appendChild(l)});
function updateReadiness(){const checked=Object.keys(state.readiness).filter(k=>state.readiness[k]).length;const moduleDone=Object.keys(state.modules).filter(k=>state.modules[k]).length;const known=Object.keys(state.known).filter(k=>state.known[k]).length;const score=Math.min(100,Math.round((checked/readiness.length*.45 + moduleDone/modules.length*.25 + Math.min(1,known/60)*.15 + Math.min(1,(state.bestExam||0)/90)*.15)*100));document.getElementById('readinessScore').textContent=score+'%';let msg='Start with the study guide, then come back here.';if(score>=90)msg='Strong preparation. Confirm your current DFPC standard/authorization and keep your practical rehearsal fresh.';else if(score>=75)msg='Close. Focus on unchecked items and your weakest exam topics.';else if(score>=50)msg='Good progress. Finish the modules, weak-card deck, and a full timed exam.';document.getElementById('readinessMessage').textContent=msg}
document.getElementById('resetInstructorProgress').addEventListener('click',()=>{if(confirm('Reset all Instructor I study progress, flashcards, and best exam score on this device?')){localStorage.removeItem(storageKey);location.reload()}});updateReadiness();
})();
